# How Long Does a New Iowa Liquor Item Last?

Every spirits item (a bottle size of a brand) that Iowa's grocery, liquor and convenience stores bought from the state between 2012 and 2025: 15,330 items, 33.3 million invoice lines. Six questions:
- how many items are on sale each month, and how many are introduced and discontinued each year;
- how long a new item lasts (Kaplan-Meier survival, with the items still on sale counted as censored, not as survivors or failures);
- what share of revenue comes from items younger than two years;
- how concentrated revenue is over items (Pareto) and whether the same items stay on top;
- how a new item's sales ramp up, in months to half of its peak;
- the trap: a month without a sale is not a discontinuation, because the "sale" here is a store's order.

The questions follow GeeksforGeeks' "Active Product Sales Analysis" (which products are active, and how that changes), answered from the same data as the sister project iowa-liquor-forecast. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/iowa-product-lifecycle/

