# Do critics' scores predict film audiences and box office?

607 film articles from the English Wikipedia (infobox figures and the Rotten Tomatoes, Metacritic and CinemaScore sentences parsed from the wikitext), joined by Wikidata to IMDb's official ratings file. Five questions:
- how critics' scores (Rotten Tomatoes, Metacritic) relate to audience ratings (IMDb, CinemaScore), and whether the gap between them depends on genre and decade;
- how much of a film's box office a critics' score explains once budget and year are known;
- how much budget and year explain of the scores themselves;
- how many figures a parser loses (and who the missing films are), and how the sample's selection (Wikipedia writes about notable films, blockbusters are over-represented, scores are as quoted at some date) bends every answer;
- the regression-to-the-mean pattern in IMDb ratings with few votes.

The task follows GeeksforGeeks' "Movies Review Scraping And Analysis". Scraping review sites is not allowed here (and IMDb's terms do not allow it), so no IMDb, Rotten Tomatoes or Metacritic page is read: the scores are the ones Wikipedia quotes, the audience ratings come from IMDb's official non-commercial datasets. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/film-reception-wikipedia/

