# Who leaves the federal workforce, and when?

Real US federal personnel data (the Office of Personnel Management's Federal Workforce Data and its Federal Employee Viewpoint Survey), aggregated and recounted. Five questions:
- who separates (quits, retirements, transfers, expired appointments, layoffs), by age band, years of service, occupation group and agency, and what happened in 2025;
- how a tenure curve looks (the share of an entering group still serving after n years), and whether it matches the real headcounts of the same cohorts later;
- how large the retirement-eligibility wave is, by agency, from age and service alone;
- how a GS employee's grade progresses with service;
- whether agencies whose staff say they are considering leaving, or are less satisfied, lose more people to quits the next year (an ecological question, with a leave-one-agency-out check and the honest traps).

The questions follow GeeksforGeeks' "HR Analytics to Track Employee Performance". The usual teaching file for it, IBM's HR attrition data, is synthetic; this uses real, public, login-free data. Only aggregates exist here: the OPM files are record-level (without names) and none of it is published. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/hr-analytics/

