# Which items do shoppers click, cart and buy next?

A real online shop's visitor events (views, add-to-carts, purchases) turned into sessions, and a session-based recommender built by hand for three objectives at once: the next click, the next cart, the next order. Five questions:
- what does the funnel from views to carts to orders look like, and how different is recommending for a purchase from recommending for a click;
- how far do popularity, popularity in a category and item-to-item co-visit counts (computed only from the past) get on each objective, and what does a blended score with stated weights say;
- what do the evaluation traps do to the numbers: training on later sessions, cutting a session at its last event, the items a shopper has already seen;
- how much of the answer is popular items, and how much is out of reach for any model trained on the past;
- can every number be recomputed without a data-science library.

The questions follow DataCamp's "Build a Multi-Objective Recommender System" (clicks, carts and orders from sessions; the original uses the OTTO dataset, which can only be downloaded from Kaggle, so a different real shop's events are used). The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/session-recommender/

