# How far can simple methods read customer reviews?

130,434 real Amazon Digital_Music reviews with star ratings: can a word list, naive Bayes or a TF-IDF logistic regression tell the positive reviews (4-5 stars) from the negative ones (1-2 stars)? And how much of the score is real? Five questions:
- how far do a lexicon, a hand-coded naive Bayes and TF-IDF + logistic regression get, against the "always positive" baseline, when 92.5% of the 1, 2, 4 and 5 star reviews are positive (accuracy against balanced accuracy and the F1 of the negative class);
- how much the score depends on how the data is split: at random, by product (no album in both halves), or by time (the newest 20% as the test);
- the leakage traps: reviews with an exact twin in the training half, the titles Amazon writes itself ("Five Stars"), reviewers and products seen in both halves;
- what a star rating means as a label: where the 3-star reviews go, and how the score moves when they are dropped, counted as negative, or counted as positive;
- what the errors look like in aggregate (by length, by negation, by contrast words, by shipping talk, by sarcasm phrases) and whether the probabilities can be trusted (calibration).

The task follows DataCamp's "Sentiment Analysis on Customer Reviews" (classify thousands of reviews as positive or negative). The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/review-sentiment/

