# Can simple detectors find New York's unusual taxi days?

Hourly yellow-taxi pickups in New York City from January 2022 to August 2026, scored against 48 labelled unusual days in the test period (federal holidays, the marathon, snowstorms, very wet days). Five questions:
- how well do four simple detectors (a rolling z-score, a seasonal-residual z-score, an IQR fence on that residual, an isolation forest) find the labelled days, by event window, with a timeliness score;
- how much does the choice of threshold matter, and what do false alarms cost per week;
- does a detector that knows the holiday calendar beat one that does not;
- what happens when past events sit in the data the baseline is learnt from;
- what did the labels leave out.

The questions follow GeeksforGeeks' "Anomaly Detection in Time Series Data", answered on the series the Numenta Anomaly Benchmark made famous (New York taxi demand) rebuilt from the original city records, with ground truth from public calendars and weather records instead of the benchmark's five hand-picked dates. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/nyc-taxi-anomalies/

