# Does ARIMA beat "same month last year"?

Monthly US retail sales of furniture stores and of office supplies, stationery and gift stores, January 1992 to July 2026, not seasonally adjusted: a time-series workflow (decomposition, stationarity tests, ARIMA/SARIMA against ETS and simple benchmarks, a walk-forward test, interval coverage) on one slow durable category and one strongly seasonal one. Five questions:
- how much of each series is trend, how much seasonality, and is the seasonality constant;
- which differencing makes them stationary, and so which ARIMA orders to try;
- whether SARIMA (or ETS, or a regression on month dummies) beats "same month last year" on months it had not seen, at 1 to 12 months ahead, and whether trading days help;
- whether the models' own 80% and 95% ranges hold, and what they said about April 2020;
- what the COVID break did to the forecasts afterwards, and whether treating the lockdown months as missing fixes it.

The task follows DataCamp's "An End-to-End Project on Time Series Analysis and Forecasting with Python" (ARIMA on furniture and office supplies sales). The original uses the Tableau Superstore sample file, which is synthetic; this uses the real Census series for the two kinds of store. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/furniture-office-sales/

