# What makes a Hacker News story reach 100 points?

703,300 Hacker News stories from 27 whole months (March, July and September of 2018 to 2026), kept as counts and metadata. Questions:
- how concentrated are the points, and how many stories reach 100 or more (the stand-in for "the front page", which this API does not show);
- whether the hour and the weekday of submission, the kind of post (Show HN, Ask HN, Tell HN, Launch HN), the title (a question mark, its length, a number) or the domain separate the 100+ stories from the rest;
- how fast a story gains points (watched live for two hours);
- how well a model that knows only what is known at submission, trained on earlier months and tested on later ones, picks the 100+ stories, and whether it is calibrated;
- how much each leakage trap (the comment count, titles edited by moderators, a domain rate computed on all months, a random split) inflates the score.

The task follows GeeksforGeeks' "News Scraping and Analysis". Scraping news sites is not allowed here, so the stand-in is Hacker News through its public search API. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/hn-front-page/

