# How fairly do votes become seats in India?

Constituency-level results of the 16 Lok Sabha general elections from 1962 to 2024, and the arithmetic of first-past-the-post: what a party's share of the votes turns into as a share of the seats. Five questions:
- how far the largest party's share of seats is above its share of votes (the seat bonus), and how far seats are from votes overall (the Gallagher index);
- how many parties the voters effectively choose between, against how many the House gets (the effective number of parties, for votes and for seats);
- how large winning margins are, how many races are close (under 5 and under 1 point), and how many winners have less than half of the votes;
- how much of that is vote splitting, and what independents, NOTA and turnout add;
- who stands and who wins: women candidates and winners.

The questions follow GeeksforGeeks' "Indian Election Data Analysis" (constituency results, parties, turnout, winners), with the indices that political scientists use for a plurality system. The page with the results and the charts is the build log:

Build log: https://mrrishit909.github.io/projects/india-elections/

